proposed_comments.body:
### Ignore a stale content-loaded activation callback (1/2)

**Severity:** `medium`

The low-priority delegate can remain queued after the fallback timer wins. That path clears `_activationPending`, but this delegate is unconditional and can call `Activate()` again after the user has switched away from Settings. Check `_activationPending` inside the queued delegate before activating. Apply it together with the fallback guard below.

```suggestion
            DispatcherQueue.TryEnqueue(DispatcherQueuePriority.Low, () =>
            {
                if (_activationPending)
                {
                    ActivatePreparedWindow();
                }
            });
```

### Ignore a stale fallback timer callback (2/2)

**Severity:** `medium`

Stopping the timer cannot withdraw a tick that has already been dispatched. If the content-loaded callback wins, it clears `_activationPending`, but an already-queued timer callback still calls `ActivatePreparedWindow()` and can activate the window again after the user has switched away. Check `_activationPending` before the fallback callback activates. Apply it together with the content-loaded guard above.

```suggestion
            if (_activationPending)
            {
                ActivatePreparedWindow();
            }
```
